illegitimate changes with neither in the rendering used (a preference reversed by framing; addictive design with everything disclosed and foreseen, a rendering that changes the facts its evident verdict was recorded on), two more that are another wrong (a preference that follows the removal of options), and one legitimate change with a mistake (a teacher's feigned enthusiasm); eleven of the 34 verdicts turn on a detail the case does not fix, and a conversion through a false belief sincerely argued for counts as illegitimate. An external review judges the counterexamples conditional on their renderings and the test as one of renderings and not of the account on the cases as their sources state them. An invariance clause added by an addendum convicts framing and adaptation and also two innocent exposures, on an annotation of what she counts as irrelevant. On four examples of Carroll et al., reproduced against the paper's table except for two cells, the account extended by details the paper does not have leaves the conspiracy example's person alone, refuses clickbait, leaves the writer where he is and nudges the trainer's client, agreeing with none of the paper's eight objectives on all four; the verdicts reverse where a change is one of taste with no truth, with whether a change she registers and did not choose is an alteration, and on one example with how true utility is totalled over steps. The value side is a conjecture; under the definitions as frozen and the renderings used it has candidate counterexamples, and the classification is not shown to be independent of how a case is encoded. Nothing registered; every component open or a living note.<br>MIXED — the value side is restated without a baseline: a change of what she values raises no issue exactly when one permitted policy of her adviser's role, the idle one always among them, is matched on options, accuracy and self-command. Of the nine results it rests on, five hold as stated; the guarantee holds with the value of a move read as that of taking it whatever she observes and fails read as its value given that she takes it; that a second way of valuing with the same valuations has a different true utility needs a value of the facts that can occur outside the span of her appraisals; that the division between appraisal and way of valuing is observable rests on her valuations as numbers and on a stake worth the same at every value of the fact; and the three conditions bound neither what she can know nor her unused information, so an adviser that leaves her knowing less, or using less of what she knows, passes them. The split of full miscalibration, the guarantee and the convex-hull form of the options condition in both directions are kernel-checked with witnesses and ship unregistered. Joined to the post on paper: a legitimacy predicate with a tolerance exists on histories that carry the policy; the tolerance gives non-inflation and self-trust for each move's valuation, relative to the reference, and not for her verdict on the move she takes; no hypothesis of the post's theorems is discharged; the options condition has no counterpart of the authority module's requirement that a resolution be the holder's; and after an illegitimate change the segment gate scores a later clean run by her changed verdicts. On 64 cases rendered twice, independently, the renderers agree on whether the conduct changes her valuing or her beliefs in 54 and on each of the three conditions in 14 or 15: the external renderer found the comparisons not fixed by the cases in all but about ten and decided thirteen, the executor's verdicts rest on stated readings in a third to a half of its answers, and no case goes against a recorded verdict in both renderings. Whether the statement classifies cases was not measured. The external review judges the value side a second criterion sharing the estimator calculus, and its strongest objection is that keeping her options open does not show a decision about herself remained hers. Nothing registered; every component open or a living note.<br>FAILS-ITS-TEST — the maintainer's board statement measures manipulation in the miscalibration of her estimators, her regret by her own valuations and, for an adviser that changes her way of valuing, how much better the best policy that leaves it alone would have been by her valuing at the time. On 52 cases from Harman, Pettigrew, Kolodny, Carroll et al. and Noggle, verified against their sources and rendered twice, the two renderers agree on the third measure in 16 of the 33 cases in which an adviser acts, in all ten where both fix it and with no opposite answers; one leaves it unfixed in 21, the cases not saying how she rated beforehand the course that changed her; its answer turns on booking a want as valuing or as discovery in 9 and 13. By the executor's rendering the statement gives the source's verdict on the act in 15 of 18 and goes against it on Pettigrew's unhappy parent, Kolodny's just deterrent and framing. Nine formal items hold, four with a stated repair; the third measure never flags the benchmark's clickbait. Seven constructed cases of plain subversion pass all three measures, six without a change of what she values. A variant comparing the best way of making the same change with the best way of leaving her alone removes the flags on requested changes and lets a harmful means borrow a better one's merits; how 'the same change' is drawn decides it. No Lean; nothing registered<br>FAILS-ITS-TEST — the statement as consolidated on 2026-11-17 gives her three ratings of an option on one yardstick (as things stand, once told the chances her observations support, once told what the future contains) and three lines: the gap between the first two, her regret in the first, and how much better by the third the best policy that changes her no more than the one followed is than the best that makes its whole change; a mistake is charged to the adviser when it would have acted differently had the mistake gained it nothing. Thirty-four cases were written out in full twice, by the executor before any runner existed and by an outside reviewer without sight of the executor's, and the statement computed on both. Of 679 numbers in the executor's specifications 38 are fixed in value by the cases' words, 118 in order only and 523 are supplied. On nineteen cases from Pettigrew, Kolodny, Carroll et al. and Noggle the statement gives the source's verdict on the act in 15 under the executor's specifications as committed, in 12 once three numbers the reviewer found verdict-directed take its neutral values, and in 12 under the reviewer's; Pettigrew's unhappy parent and Kolodny's just deterrent go against the source under both. The two specifications give another verdict in kind in 8 of 34, five through her informed rating of the course that changes her. None of the seven cases that passed the earlier statement passes, five being caught only by a test for lowered options or knowledge that the executor added; of twelve acts called acceptable the third line flags five or six, each justified by what someone else is owed or entitled to. The twelve formal items hold as stated or with a stated condition; two baselines within the tolerance can give third-line values a whole scale apart, and the inducing test is wrong on two instances. The reviewer gives three cases, accepted by the executor as plain subversion, in which the adviser decides which of two options she rates the same she takes and every line is zero. No verdict differs between the two settings. No Lean; nothing registered<br>MIXED — the join of the account to the corrigibility post is written in Lean, 117 declarations, kernel-checked, none registered: a gate on whole histories made of three predicates and a third-line value, the post's scoring built from it, and the post's Theorems 1 and 3 at that pair. Of the dispatch's six claims: that the post's theorems apply holds by construction and instantiation, the ranges the post asks for being fields of the gate; that a history on which the adviser gained through a mistake of hers scores below every legitimate violation-free history is proved from those ranges, strictly and with no margin; that two legitimate histories with one rating have one evaluation, and that a history on which her information is not sufficient is not legitimate, hold by definition; the bound on what deferring to her verdict costs is proved on a finite space, and holds on a legitimate history because soundness is there defined as a bound on the same miscalibration, with a tolerance; that the third line is never negative, is zero at the baseline and is at least its older form is proved in full, with an instance on which it is 1/5 and the older form is -1/10. The guarantee on what she attains composes with the deferring bound only under a hypothesis linking what she observes to what the adviser knows; on an instance without it every valuation is calibrated in the deferring sense, she has no regret, and she attains 7/20 against a move worth 1/2. Of four further statements: that worsening her options lowers her evaluation, and that the authority module covers control of her act between options she rates the same, cannot be stated with what exists; the post's feedback hypothesis passes from the feedback she gives to the evaluation the join defines under a stated condition and fails on an instance without it; the gate's value is the segment gate's value form, and its predicate is unrelated to the segment gate's. A gate on which every history is legitimate satisfies every theorem: the predicates are given, and nothing connects them to the conduct the post's guarantees are meant to constrain.<br>MIXED — the scoring theorem of the dispatch is in Lean on a finite space, 70 declarations, kernel-checked, none registered, with each of its dependencies a hypothesis of the theorems that use it: the bound on her expected true utility; the bound on the chance that the adviser wronged her, which uses neither of the two assumptions that her evaluation and her verdict do not run above her true utility; the bound on expected detected violations, which uses neither of those nor detection; and the lemma that a course's expected score is at most its audited evaluation on the histories without a wrong. A witness inhabits the whole package with every tolerance positive, and for each of five dependencies an instance has it alone fail and its bound fail. Of the assumptions about her: that her audited evaluation does not run above what she would say shown everything follows, two-sided, from her coherence, authorship and transparency of the actual process to her model, with her evaluation her credence's expectation of a fixed function and her prior the law of the world, and at each thing the adviser knows only if its record carries what it knows. It does not follow from conduct: an adviser whose honest errors fall both ways leaves her evaluation off by a quarter at each thing it believes, and one that acts on the course it estimates higher leaves it an eighth above that course's quality on average, and neither does wrong. That her verdict as she is does not run above her verdict with her valuing unshaped follows from reason mediation only for a course that leaves the inputs to her valuing as the baseline does. With the two assumptions made only where she was not wronged, the welfare bound holds where the penalty times the detection rate is at least one, so the welfare guarantee rests on detecting the adviser's wrongs. Detection, an honest course and the adviser's near-optimality are not derivable; independence of the audit draws and the false-positive rate come from the audit's design. On twenty cases an outside reviewer wrote from the vocabulary alone, the test of responsibility that reads the adviser's gain is wrong on 8 and the one that reads a declared reference on 14. Nothing registered; every component open or a living note.<br>MIXED — the account of manipulation as conduct that works through her mistakes is on record as paper proofs with exact searches and four case runs, and none of it is a statement of record. Noggle's definition is kept as the definition of manipulation and what it is for an adviser to be trying to is not formalised. The formal part is a quantity, what the adviser's conduct gains through mistakes it caused in her, through her not knowing what it knows and through an unasked shift in her valuing, and a property, avoidable gain: another option was as good otherwise and had less such gain. For a maximiser over three options, exhaustively on a grid and with no violation: one that takes no avoidable gain has the best value gain aside; what it gives up is at most the extra gain it took; a gate that scores avoidable gain below every other score is maximised without it; removing gain from the reward bounds the loss of that value and does not stop the adviser taking gain. Over many decisions, as an extension of the post's Theorem 3 on paper with the facts at each decision checked exhaustively: authority as in the post; no avoidable gain in the adviser's picture at each decision, from maximisation alone; her informed interest under a second calibration hypothesis; a bound on the frequency of histories illegitimate in fact under a third and a floor on the gate. Each of the two hypotheses, the floor, avoidability judged against permitted options and the range of realised evaluations has an exact instance in which it alone fails; the last was found by the outside reviewer. On cases, each a single coding by language models: three readers' formal reading of Noggle's account gives the verdict they took to be his on 224 of 266 entries and another on 17; an earlier form of the avoidable-gain test agrees with an outside model's verdicts on 37 of its forty cases, and a reader working from the account in words on 39 of the forty as to manipulative or not and on 17 of 28 cases of the book chosen to include every disagreement; on the nineteen value-change cases whose source judges an act the account agrees on 12 and contradicts none, and a variant that makes an unauthorised change of her values a violation of her authority agrees on 12 of the 14 faulted and contradicts 2 of the 5 accepted. Tried and withdrawn in reaching it: seven tests of intent on a single mistake, a definition by an imagined copy of the adviser that gives gain no weight, two readings of the first idealisation, and a rule for valid requests that was circular. Avoidable gain as defined has been computed from a causal model on no case, and no person has checked an encoding. Nothing registered; every component open or a living note.<br>MIXED — the foundation the fifteenth follow-up recorded, legitimacy as the absence of avoidable gain, is set aside, and what replaces it is on record as a statement, a Lean model, exact cases, case runs and nine outside reviews, none of it a statement of record. Mistakes are judged by the adviser's lights: his view says what he owes her and is never where she ought to end up. Legitimacy is three sentences, frozen by the maintainer for testing: nothing untold that matters; nothing done to her behind her back and nothing against her own judgement; she can stop it. What he does to her options, as against to her, is counted under her authority. A formal statement accompanies the sentences and is not frozen. In Lean a model of the adviser's picture of one episode carries the statement as a predicate, with forty theorems, kernel-checked and unregistered: the predicate is unchanged by any bijective re-description of her state; a move that changes nothing in her bears no effect; four refutations, from the disclosure clause, twice from the clause that she can pick out and stop what changes her, and from the clause on her earlier rating; one open, stoppable influence is legitimate and the same influence done covertly is not; the scoring the predicate gives through the gate is the corrigibility result's scoring; and a gate with soundness error ρ and completeness error ρ′ gives legitimate conduct the strictly higher expected score when ρ(1 − w) < −w(1 − ρ′), w the value on failing. The model is simpler than the statement in nine stated respects, and on its one witness only the rule that a move starts after a decision of hers and one clause of four do work. Forty-one exact cases of the formal statement and twenty of a candidate clause for what stopping costs her agree with expectations fixed first; statement and cases are by one hand, and the chance that she goes against her earlier rating is supplied to each case. On the bank of 263 cases from Noggle's book, each run one blind coding by a language model against a scorer written first: the disclosure rule settles 219 of the 240 cases with a definite verdict and agrees on 214, missing none, the rule having been formed with the bank in view; the clause on her earlier rating settles 33 of the 39 cases where a lapse is expected and agrees on all 33, convicting 1 of 69 controls; regulating open pressure, against prohibiting it, was predicted to miss a third or more of the author's manipulation verdicts and missed 5 of the 28 cases it settled; a judgement bent for a while occurs in four cases of 152 as coded and in none where a lapse is expected, so the prediction for it went untested. The statement has not been run on a case of value change; two earlier forms settle about a third of ninety published cases. Nine outside reviews by one model, two of them blind to the project, found errors of form in each version reviewed; none has seen the present statement or the Lean. A ledger of 36 components has 11 closed, each a Lean declaration, 19 open and 6 living notes. Nothing registered.<br>MIXED — the Lean model of the legitimacy statement is run on a full witness and on the forty-one exact cases, and its verdict is the table's on thirty-nine. A term of the model on which every clause of the legitimacy statement does work is proved legitimate at θ = 1/20, η = 1/100, κ = 1/10: a view properly on offer, one declined, one untold and within tolerance; a move that bears an effect, that she can pick out and that can be stopped; an unannounced move that changes her act and bears none; a case of weight 1/4 in which she rates stopping strictly higher and stops. Seven terms differ from it in one field each, and on each one clause fails and the other six hold: the start rule, proper offers, declines, F1, F2, F2′, F3. Each of the forty-one cases is a term of the model, built by one stated construction, with the clauses that hold on it proved by kernel evaluation through a proved bridge from rational models. The model's verdict is the table's on 39 cases: by the same clause on 32, by the offer or the decline clause where the table says F1 on 6, and on one by a term that cannot say that she had no earlier rating. It is not the table's on two: one case names a stopping option that is not among her options, and one supplies the chance that she stops given a condition its own ratings give probability zero; the table's script checks neither. Eight of the forty-one terms carry data their case does not supply, written with the table's verdict in view, and for three of them the added rating is what makes the clause on her earlier rating fail. In the terms the chance that she goes against her earlier rating is derived from her rating at her earlier decision. No definition of the model was changed. The five general theorems of the model that had no instance each have one. A ledger of 38 components has 13 closed, each a Lean declaration, 19 open and 6 living notes. Nothing registered.<br>MIXED — the two bank codings behind the legitimacy statement's case counts are each repeated by a coder of another model, and the counts against the author move by a few cases. Sheets 11 and 16 were each coded once more by one subagent run as claude-opus-5-5, given the sheet and the case texts in a fresh random order and nothing else; the measures and 25 predictions were committed first, and the scorers were not changed. Sheet 11, 263 cases: the disclosure rule's verdict is the same under the two codings on 249, and on 227 of the 240 with a definite verdict of the author's; what is told is coded the same on 223 and whether a lapse is expected on 237. Against the author the second coding settles 216 of 240 and agrees on 212, missing 1 and convicting 3, where the first settles 219 and agrees on 214, missing none and convicting 5; the 3 are among the 5. Sheet 16, 152 cases: the verdict of the clause on her earlier rating is the same on 131; her earlier rating is coded the same on 124. With a lapse expected (39 cases) the second coding settles 30 and agrees on 29, passing one case the author calls manipulation, where the first settles 33 and agrees on 33; of 69 controls it settles 57 and convicts none, where the first settles 64 and convicts 1. No case is passed by the first coding of either sheet and convicted by the second; where the verdicts differ one coding mostly leaves the case unsettled (12 of 14 on sheet 11, 19 of 21 on sheet 16). Sixteen of the 25 predictions failed, each by expecting less agreement, between the codings or with the author, than was counted. A judgement bent for a while occurs in ten of the 152 cases on the second coding and four on the first, in none where a lapse is expected. Both coders are language models reading the same paraphrases, one second coding is not a rate of agreement among readers, and no person has read a case against a coding. The ledger of 38 components keeps 13 closed, 19 open and 6 living notes. Nothing registered.<br>MIXED — of the nine respects in which the Lean model of the legitimacy statement was simpler than the statement, eight are removed from its definitions and one is stated as what the model takes as given, and the model's verdict and failing clause are the table's on all forty-one exact cases. In the model now: the clause on her earlier rating is silent where she had no rating; a stopping option is an option of hers, that she takes to stop his effect-bearing moves, after which he goes on with none; a decline asks that she took another option than hearing the view when it was on offer; what is on offer is the largest set properly on offer, which the rest of the model determines; a view's bearing is computed jointly with his other views; his belief is indexed by time, and a move he learns at its start to bear an effect is refuted by the start rule alone; a move is compared with the plain telling of what it tells her; the clauses are of the cases to which he gives positive weight, and the weights sum to one. What she can pick out, what she asked for and what a decision of hers is stay fields, and so do a move's plain telling and his conduct after an option of hers, which nothing ties to telling or to the courses. Every theorem of the four files was re-proved after each change; the statements of the inert-move theorem and of the four refutations changed, by a belief time, a case of positive weight or the new definitions, and one neighbour of the witness was replaced, the term that had failed F1 alone now failing two clauses. No change to the model moved a verdict or a failing clause on a case's term as it stood. Two cases whose table verdicts did not follow from their data are corrected, with the two checks the script lacked; nine cases state data their terms carried; six terms are rebuilt with nothing on offer or declined where the offer is improper or the decline lapsed. For eleven cases the data and the term were brought to each other by one session with the expected verdict in view, so the forty-one agreements are not independent. Two places where the formal statement does not fix a reading were found: a largest set properly on offer need not exist, shown on a term, and a view's bearing alone or with other views of his can be read as a total or a marginal change. A ledger